# Password Hashing

Never store passwords in plain text. Always hash with bcrypt at cost factor (rounds) **≥ 12**. Never use MD5, SHA-1, or other fast general-purpose hashes for passwords — they are too cheap to brute-force.

## Do / Don't

```typescript
import bcrypt from "bcrypt";

// ❌ BAD — plaintext or fast hashes; unsuitable for passwords
await db.users.insert({ email, password });
crypto.createHash("md5").update(password).digest("hex");
crypto.createHash("sha1").update(password).digest("hex");
bcrypt.hash(password, 8); // cost too low

// ✅ GOOD — bcrypt with cost ≥ 12; store only the hash
const passwordHash = await bcrypt.hash(password, 12);
await db.users.insert({ email, passwordHash });
const ok = await bcrypt.compare(password, passwordHash);
```

## Checklist

- Never persist or return the raw password after the request is handled.
- Use a well-maintained bcrypt library (`bcrypt` / `bcryptjs`); do not roll custom hashing.
- Cost factor must be at least `12`; raise it if threat model or hardware allows without hurting login UX.
- Store only the bcrypt hash (which embeds salt + cost); never store plaintext or unsalted digests.
- Do not “upgrade” legacy MD5/SHA1 password columns by hashing those digests again without a proper migration that re-hashes the raw password on next login.
